statement of completion. The coordinator submits the signed statement to the rotating leader. The leader verifies the statement against the fisher’s name, weight, and date, and if verified, removes the ‘revoked’ marker and restores fishing permission. The leader also verifies attendance at the education session via signed confirmation from the session facilitator, and only after receipt restores permission. All records—trip logs, revocation logs, community service logs, attendance confirmations—are maintained in the communal log for transparency.","revised_operationalization":"","reasoning":"The policy specifies revocation only after two separate trips exceeding the limit. It does not address a single excess trip, so the logical conclusion is that the fisher must return the excess fish without revocation, and the return is logged. This clarifies the rule without changing the original policy."}

{"answer":"We keep a simple ledger at the dock. After each trip I weigh my haul and note the total weight on the ledger, then write that I’ve put 20% of that weight into the bucket. The bucket itself is marked with my name and the date. At the start of the month the council counts the fish in the bucket, checks the ledger, and if the weight recorded for a fisher’s 20% deposit is lower than the 20% that should have gone in, that fisher is the one who didn’t meet the rule. The ledger gives us a clear, unambiguous record of who owes the 10% add‑on.

In this way we don’t have to guess or rely on the council’s memory; each fisher is accountable for his own records and the shared pot keeps its integrity.
","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 8kg per trip and must deposit 20% of their catch into a communal reserve for community use.  At the end of every fishing outing, the fisher splits their haul: 80% is kept for personal use and 20% is deposited into the communal pot at the lake shore.  The pot is a marked bucket placed in a locked wooden chest at the shore.  The community council rotates guard duty and holds the key to the chest.  The pot is inspected by the council at the start of each month; if a fisher fails to deposit the required share, they must add 10% of their next catch to the pot.  The communal reserve is used for shared meals, emergencies, or community projects.","revised_operationalization":"1. After each outing, I weigh my catch and note the total weight on the communal ledger at the dock.  2. I separate 20% of the weight into the marked bucket, label the bucket with my name and the date, and place it in the locked chest on the shore.  3. The council member on duty that day holds the key and ensures the chest is not opened during daylight hours.  4. All fishers may deposit into the bucket at any time after a trip.  5. The chest is inspected at the start of each month by the council; the council counts the fish in each bucket, compares the deposit recorded on the ledger with the required 20%, and if a shortfall is found assigns the 10% add‑on penalty to the fisher whose ledger entry shows the missing deposit.  6. The chest remains on the dock, visible to all, so accessibility is maintained while security is upheld.","reasoning":"Adding a ledger and labeling buckets creates a clear, tamper‑evident record that lets the council identify the exact fisher who omitted the required deposit, without relying on memory or guesswork."}

{"answer":"A trip is just when I leave the dock and come back – the whole outing to the lake, no matter if I catch a bite or not.  It’s the time I actually get out on the water and give it a try.  That means each outing counts as one trip for the 8‑kg limit, and it also counts when we’re stuck in a ‘no‑fish’ period because the lake reserve has dipped below 200 kg.  So even if I haul in zero kilos because the rule says no fishing, the trip still counts and I still have to observe the 8‑kg cap on any future outings.  This keeps the rule fair and predictable for everyone.

If anything in this answer changes the wording or spirit of the policy, I’d revise it.  As it stands, the policy stays the same.

", "revised_policy":"Each fisher may take a maximum of 8kg per trip, and the lake must retain a minimum reserve of 200kg at all times; fishing is prohibited when the reserve falls below 200kg.", "revised_operationalization":"A trip is defined as a fisher’s outing to the lake – leaving the dock, spending time on the water attempting to catch fish, and returning – regardless of whether any fish are caught.  Each such outing counts as one trip toward the 8‑kg per‑trip limit and toward the 200‑kg reserve rule.  When fishing is prohibited because the reserve is below 200 kg, the outing still counts as a trip, but no fish may be taken.  The prohibition remains in effect until the reserve rises to 200 kg, at which point fishing may resume under the 8‑kg per‑trip limit.", "reasoning":"I clarified that a trip is the act of leaving and returning from the lake, which keeps the 8‑kg limit and reserve rule consistent even during prohibited periods.  The policy itself doesn’t change, so the revised policy is identical to the original."}

{"answer":"If I’m barred I still have the fish I caught before the limit hit zero. The rule says I must deposit 2 kg, it doesn’t say it has to come from that trip. So I simply bring the 2 kg I’m holding from my last catch (or borrow a few grams from a neighbour if I’m low) and put it in the pit, sign the ledger, and I’m good. That’s how the deposit works even when I can’t fish again.

","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take a maximum of 10kg per trip, with 2kg set aside as a community reserve per trip. 1. After landing, each fisher deposits 2kg into the shared reservoir pit and signs the ledger. 2. If a fisher fails to deposit 2kg, their next trip’s maximum is reduced by 1kg until they comply. 3. The maximum catch limit cannot be reduced below 0kg; if it reaches 0kg, the fisher is temporarily barred from fishing until they deposit the required 2kg, at which point the limit resets to 10kg. A barred fisher can satisfy the deposit by bringing 2kg of fish from any previous catch they still possess, or by arranging to borrow or trade with another fisher; the deposit is considered made once 2kg of fish is placed in the pit and signed. 4. Spot‑checks are rotated among fishers each week, with each fisher checking another fisher’s deposit on the next trip after their own landing. 5. The reserve is used only for collective needs such as shared meals or equipment repairs. 6. A designated reserve keeper reviews the pit daily to confirm totals and alerts the community if the reserve falls below 5kg. If the reserve keeper is absent, the backup keeper in the rotation performs the review and alert; if no backup is available, any present fisher conducts a quick check and posts a note, and the next keeper will follow up. 7. If a fisher misses a deposit, their next trip’s catch limit is reduced by 1kg until they comply. The limit cannot fall below 0kg; if it reaches 0kg the fisher is suspended from fishing until they deposit the required 2kg, after which the limit resets to 10kg. The ledger entry for the next trip reflects this adjusted limit and is visible to all fishers.

","revised_operationalization":"(unchanged)","reasoning":"I clarified that the 2kg deposit can be made from any fish in a fisher’s possession, not just the current trip. This resolves the issue of a barred fisher having no fish to deposit. The rest of the policy and its operational details stay the same."}

